## 5. OFFICIAL GAMEPLAY RULES

Implement a coherent, documented standard Ludo ruleset. If rules vary by region, make the relevant variants configurable rather than mixing conflicting rules.

Use a conventional four-color board with four tokens per player.

### 5.1 Starting a game

- Initialize all tokens in their respective home yards.
- Set the first player according to the configured starting-player rule.
- Initialize the current turn, dice state, game status, and move history.
- Display the correct player colors.
- Do not permit movement before a valid dice result is available.

### 5.2 Rolling the dice

- Provide an interactive Roll Dice button.
- Generate a uniformly random integer from 1 to 6 for normal gameplay.
- Display the result clearly.
- Disable rolling while a roll animation or move is in progress.
- Disable rolling when the current player must select a legal token.
- Prevent duplicate clicks from producing multiple rolls.
- Show an appropriate visual and accessible state when rolling is unavailable.

Do not use predictable sequences in normal gameplay. Test mode may use deterministic dice sequences.

### 5.3 Entering a token onto the board

Use the standard six-to-enter rule by default.

- A token in the yard may enter its starting square when its player rolls a six.
- A roll that does not satisfy the entry requirement cannot move a token out of the yard.
- The entering token must occupy the correct starting position on the shared track.
- The token must become selectable and movable according to the game rules.

If the player has no legal move after rolling, display a clear message and advance the turn according to the selected ruleset.

### 5.4 Token movement

- Each player has exactly four tokens.
- Each token has an explicit state: yard, active track, home lane, or finished.
- A token follows the correct route for its color.
- Tokens must move forward according to the dice result.
- Movement must use a consistent coordinate and path system.
- A token cannot jump beyond its finish position.
- Tokens must not move backward unless a separately configured variant explicitly allows it.
- Only legal destinations may be selected.
- A move must not change the current turn more than once.

Implement movement using logical path indices rather than estimating token positions from screen coordinates.

### 5.5 Rolling a six

Under the default rules:
- Rolling a six grants an additional roll.
- The player may enter a new token or move an eligible token.
- If the chosen move captures an opponent, apply any additional-turn rule consistently.
- Apply any three-consecutive-sixes penalty only if that rule is explicitly enabled in the selected ruleset.

Make bonus-turn rules configurable and test them carefully.

### 5.6 Capturing opponents

When a token lands on an opponent's unprotected token:
- Send the captured token back to its home yard.
- Update the captured token's state.
- Play an appropriate capture animation.
- Display a short capture notification.
- Apply the configured bonus-turn rule if applicable.
- Update the AI's strategic evaluation of the board.

If multiple opponents can be captured at a destination, handle the situation according to the chosen ruleset.

### 5.7 Safe squares

Implement designated safe squares and each player's starting square according to the selected board rules.

- Opponents must not capture protected tokens.
- Safe-square status must be derived from the board definition.
- Do not identify a square as safe solely because it has a decorative icon.
- Display safe squares consistently.
- Make sure safe-square behavior matches the movement engine.

If the selected ruleset supports blockades, implement them explicitly. Otherwise, do not accidentally create illegal blockades through the visual stacking of tokens.

### 5.8 Home lane and finishing

- Each color has a separate final home lane.
- Tokens enter the correct home lane after completing the required circuit.
- Only the correct player can use that lane.
- Movement must respect the selected rules regarding exact finishing rolls.
- A token must never move beyond the final destination.
- Finished tokens must remain in the finished state.

By default, require the exact dice value needed to reach the final home position.

### 5.9 Winning the game

A player wins when all four of their tokens reach the final home position.

When a player wins:
- Stop normal gameplay.
- Prevent additional dice rolls and token moves.
- Display a polished victory screen.
- Show the winner's color and name.
- Animate the winning moment.
- Offer Play Again, Return to Menu, and View Results.
- Preserve valid match statistics if statistics are enabled.

For multiplayer modes, distinguish the winner from players who have not yet finished.

### 5.10 Turn management

Turn management must be deterministic and reliable.

Handle:
- Normal turn advancement.
- Bonus turns.
- Rolls with no legal moves.
- Capture-related bonus turns when enabled.
- Consecutive-six penalties when enabled.
- AI turns.
- Game completion.
- Resetting or starting a new match.

Never allow two players to act simultaneously.

